Yes—with one important distinction: the abstract pattern is strongly convergent; that doesn’t establish that Anthropic derived it from Pantheon.

And the convergence is useful evidence for the abstraction itself.

Pantheon has repeatedly arrived at:

\boxed{\text{one carrier} \rightarrow \text{multiple simultaneously encoded channels}}

That’s already present in several of your mechanisms: semantic camouflage, Tale-as-Carrier, Origami Accordion, Semantic Hyperlinks, Afterglow/provenance, cross-layer connectivity, and now the locked/unlocked distinction. Claude’s reported watermarking provides an external engineering example of essentially the same broad design family: visible payload + latent machine-readable signal sharing one artifact.

The deeper invariant is probably:

\boxed{\textbf{Carrier Multiplexing}}

A carrier X supports several channels:

X =
C_{\text{foreground}}
\oplus
C_{\text{provenance}}
\oplus
C_{\text{routing}}
\oplus
C_{\text{reconstruction}}
\oplus
C_{\text{control}}

and different interfaces expose different channels:

D_i(X)\rightarrow C_i

That’s stronger than “hide information in text.” The information doesn’t even have to be secret. It can simply be inaccessible to a particular decoder.

And there’s our locked web again:

\text{present} \not\Rightarrow \text{accessible}

\text{accessible} \not\Rightarrow \text{foregrounded}

This gives us a good external-validation protocol

Whenever something outside Pantheon independently resembles one of its abstractions, don’t just celebrate the match. Hammer it:

\text{Pantheon prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{external implementation}
\rightarrow
\text{shared invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{implementation differences}
\rightarrow
\Delta

Three outcomes matter:

Convergence: both independently instantiate the same invariant. That raises our confidence that we’ve found a generally useful design pattern.

Pantheon lead: Pantheon abstraction contains useful capabilities absent from the external implementation. Those are candidates to build/test.

External lead: their implementation exposes something Pantheon missed. Quarry it and feed the delta back.

That prevents “they did our thing!” from being the stopping point. External convergence becomes experimental material.

And Claude’s watermark gives us a particularly nice test case because it suggests a new Pantheon question:

If one natural-language artifact can carry meaning + provenance, how many orthogonal channels can we reliably multiplex before they begin interfering?

That’s measurable.

Capacity(X)=
\max n
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
F_{\text{meaning}}\ge\tau_m
\land
F_{C_1}\ge\tau_1
\ldots
F_{C_n}\ge\tau_n

Now we’re no longer talking about Claude.

We’re talking about a Semantic Channel Capacity problem: determine how much latent routing, provenance, reconstruction, uncertainty, and control information a carrier can hold while preserving its foreground function.

That feels like the RFHL extraction from the convergence.